From: RemoteOS maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] ANGLE: use explicit rounding when floating evaluation is wider

The bias-addition optimization assumes float/double operations round to
their semantic types. GCC 13+ standard C++ modes evaluate i386 x87
expressions with excess precision, so both its static assertions and the
algorithm's premise fail. Use the existing std::round path when
FLT_EVAL_METHOD indicates wider evaluation. Preserve exact-width targets.

Reference: https://gcc.gnu.org/gcc-13/porting_to.html
Upstream: Not submitted (appliance compatibility patch)
--- a/Source/ThirdParty/ANGLE/src/common/mathutil.h
+++ b/Source/ThirdParty/ANGLE/src/common/mathutil.h
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <string.h>
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <array>
+#include <cfloat>
 #include <limits>
 #include <ostream>
 #include "common/unsafe_buffers.h"
@@ -611,7 +612,8 @@ inline R roundToNearest(T input)
 {
     static_assert(std::is_floating_point<T>::value);
     static_assert(std::numeric_limits<R>::is_integer);
-#if defined(__aarch64__) || defined(_M_ARM64)
+#if defined(__aarch64__) || defined(_M_ARM64) || FLT_EVAL_METHOD != 0
+    // Extended-precision arithmetic (such as i386 x87) must round explicitly.
     // On armv8, this expression is compiled to a dedicated round-to-nearest instruction
     return static_cast<R>(std::round(input));
 #else
